body:
The bundle was wrapped in a large leather bag. Inside were two carved wooden snuffing tablets, a carved snuffing tube with human-hair braids, a pair of llama-bone spatulas, a polychrome textile band, dried plant fragments tied with fiber and wool strings, and a pouch sewn from three fox snouts. The PNAS release summarizes the date directly: "Radiocarbon dating traced the bundle to 905-1170 CE."

{{figure:bundle}}

These objects matter because they are not merely symbolic. Snuffing tablets, tubes, and spatulas are part of a recognizable Andean and Amazonian technology for preparing and ingesting powdered psychoactive plants. The fox-snout pouch is the key tested object: scrapings from its interior produced the chemical evidence that anchors the interpretation.

{{figure:pouch}}

The assemblage also cautions against flattening the find into a single drug name. It appears to preserve a small ritual pharmacopoeia, not a single plant.
